      "id": 1,
      "text": "Orla was founded in 1890 as a railroad section house for the Pecos Valley Railroad.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        12,
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orla%2C_Texas"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s name, Spanish for “border,” reflects its position where the Atchison, Topeka and Santa Fe line met the Texas frontier.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://unitedstatesghosttowns.com/orla-texas-ghost-town/"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "During the 1960s oil boom, Orla’s population swelled to about 250 residents, a sharp rise from just ten in 1933.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://unitedstatesghosttowns.com/orla-texas-ghost-town/"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Today, only two people live in Orla, while abandoned wooden buildings and shuttered businesses stand as silent witnesses to its former prosperity.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://unitedstatesghosttowns.com/orla-texas-ghost-town/"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The town’s historic structures include a weathered gas station, old grocery store, and modern petroleum worker lodging amid the abandoned buildings.",
